     "text": "Printed by: ROI_12 11/09/2026\nCONFIDENTIAL - Contains PHI\nMalone Shields and Kelly Medical Group | Dr. Courtney Dennis, pulmonology\nPatient: Johnathan Hernandez DOB: 01/12/1967 Sex: M MRN: 622735\n\nPROBLEM LIST\n - Asthma [controlled] onset 07/2019\n\nMEDICATIONS\n albuterol 90 mcg\n\nENCOUNTERS\n\n--- 02/12/2026 Office Visit ---\nS: Patient Johnathan Hernandez follows up for evaluation and management of a rash. He reports that the rash appeared several weeks ago and has been persistent despite the use of over-the-counter topical steroids. He denies any systemic symptoms such as fever or itching.\n\nO: On physical examination, the rash appears as erythematous patches on the patient's forearms and chest. There is no evidence of warmth, swelling, or drainage. Vital signs are as follows: BP 137/80, Wt 158 lb, HR 72 bpm, RR 16 breaths/min, Temp 98.6°F. The cardiovascular, respiratory, and abdominal examinations are unremarkable. The neurologic examination is also within normal limits.\n\nA: The patient presents with a stable rash that has not with over-the-counter treatments. Given the chronicity and persistent nature of the rash, it is prudent to consider a dermatologic consultation.\n\nP: Continue current management of the rash with the prescribed over-the-counter topical steroids. Advise the patient to monitor for any changes in the rash. Recommend a follow-up appointment in 3-6 months or sooner if symptoms worsen. Referral to a dermatologist has been made for further evaluation and management.\n\n---\n\n--- 07/14/2024 Office Visit ---\nS: Patient Johnathan Hernandez follows up for an ankle sprain that occurred approximately one month ago during a recreational soccer game. He reports persistent pain and difficulty with weight-bearing activities. He denies any swelling, redness, or additional injuries.\n\nO: On physical examination, the patient has mild tenderness and swelling around the lateral malleolus of the right ankle. There is no visible ecchymosis or open wound. Range of motion is limited due to pain. Vital signs are as follows: BP 132/74, Wt 170 lb, HR 68 bpm, RR 14 breaths/min, Temp 98.4°F. The cardiovascular, respiratory, and abdominal examinations are unremarkable. The neurologic examination is also within normal limits.\n\nA: The patient continues to experience symptoms from a right ankle sprain with persistent pain and limited mobility. Despite the chronicity, there are no signs of acute injury or Continue with RICE (Rest, Ice, Compression, Elevation) protocol. Prescribe acetaminophen 500 mg as needed for pain. Recommend ph"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "ysical therapy referral for weight-bearing exercises and ankle strengthening. Advise the patient to avoid activities that exacerbate pain. Schedule a follow-up appointment in 3-6 months or sooner if symptoms persist or worsen.\n\n---\n\n--- 12/13/2022 Office Visit ---\nS: Patient Johnathan Hernandez follows up for his asthma, which has been managed with albuterol 90 mcg as needed. He reports occasional shortness of breath and wheezing, particularly in the early morning and at He denies any recent exacerbations or hospitalizations.\n\nO: On physical examination, the patient appears well and is in no acute distress. The lungs are clear to auscultation with no wheezing, rhonchi, or rales. Vital signs are as follows: BP 135/78, Wt 160 lb, HR 78 RR 18 breaths/min, Temp 98.2°F. The cardiovascular, abdominal, and neurologic examinations are unremarkable.\n\nA: The patient's asthma is currently well-controlled, with no recent exacerbations or emergency department visits. He continues to use albuterol as needed for symptom management.\n\nP: Continue with albuterol g0 mcg as needed for breakthrough symptoms. Review proper inhaler technique and adherence to the prescribed regimen. Recommend a follow-up appointment in 3-6 months or sooner if symptoms worsen or if there are any changes in asthma control.\n\n---\n\nSocial History: The patient is a former smoker, having smoked one pack per day since July 1998. He quit smoking in January 2024. There is no reported alcohol use.\n\nFamily History: The patient's mother passed away from breast cancer at age 46 and had Alzheimer's disease diagnosed at age 65. His father had a stroke and passed away at age 56.\n\nPage 6 of 6\n"
